ent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating Shakespeare context on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Strong answer: Accurate, sourced-from-class facts; strategy practical (paraphrase, gloss). Reasoning: I used Shakespeare context by attending to theatre conditions rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with Shakespeare context, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Claiming Shakespeare is ''random old nonsense''. It is patterned language you can learn. Correction: Strong answer: Accurate, sourced-from-class facts; strategy practical (paraphrase, gloss). Why: the correction restores accurate use of Shakespeare context.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s4-eng-y7-w17-l1","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise Shakespeare context.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to t