-air playhouses, daylight performance, boy actors in many female roles in his period) and the fact that Early Modern English differs from ours. Use class-approved editions/excerpts; focus on meaning-making, not mock accents.","A claim unrelated to the text.","An answer copied without understanding."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about Shakespeare context?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nContext note: plays written for performance; soliloquies share thoughts with audience. Language: thee/thou, inverted word order. Strategy: paraphrase chunk by chunk.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite four context facts from class sources and one language strategy you will use when reading.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Accurate, sourced-from-class facts; strategy practical (paraphrase, gloss).","explain":"Studying Shakespeare includes historical theatre context (open-air playhouses, daylight performance, boy actors in many female roles in his period) and the fact that Early Modern English differs from ours. Use class-approved editions/excerpts; focus on meaning-making, not mock accents.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Claiming Shakespeare is ''random old nonsense''. It is patterned language you can learn. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Shakespeare context.","caption":"Paraphrase chunks.",